    "what_this_means": "Grisham's night was the difference, but the lineup's timing is becoming the story. They scored in four separate innings. Chasing early, leveling in the middle, breaking through in the seventh, then pulling away in the eighth. This is not a team that needs to lead from the start. It's a team that trusts itself to answer. Will Warren lasted four innings, but the bullpen did not let that early exit become an excuse. Five relievers combined for 4.1 scoreless innings. That held the door open for the bats to come through when it mattered. Grisham carried the offensive load with two home runs and a go-ahead double in the seventh, but the real signal here is consistency. Thirteen hits across the lineup, converging when the game was closest. This works until the pitching stops bailing them out early. They won by five, but they trailed after three innings.",
